          "source_quote": "καὶ εἰ κρείττων ἔσται ἢ οὐδὲ ἔσται",
          "english_quote": "and whether it will be better or will no longer exist.",
          "issue": "“No longer” substitutes a temporal contrast for the emphatic negation οὐδὲ: the alternative is that the world will not even exist. Although cessation is contextually understandable, the draft loses the explicit contrast between being better and not existing at all.",
          "suggested_revision": "Render: “and whether it will be better or will not even exist.”",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "καὶ ἵνα μὴ τὸ κατ᾿ εἶδος λέγω, ταῦτά τε καὶ τὰ τούτοις ἑπόμενα μαθεῖν εἶπον θέλειν.",
          "english_quote": "And, not to speak item by item, I said that I wanted to learn these things and those that follow from them.”",
          "issue": "The closing quotation mark places the retrospective report εἶπον θέλειν inside Clement’s earlier direct speech. The Greek resumes narration by reporting what he said he wanted; the draft instead presents that retrospective report as an additional utterance to his interlocutor.",
          "suggested_revision": "Close the direct quotation after the world’s possible nonexistence. Continue outside it: “And, to avoid speaking item by item, I said that I wanted to learn these things and those that follow from them.”",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
